{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>He guided his hands, for, etc.</b> The word ״כִּי״ is said, and its sense is to the contrary — for in spite of this, etc. Some have explained this in this manner: ״כִּי מְנַשֶּׁה הַבְּכוֹר״ — that is, Menasheh being the firstborn; yet nevertheless, etc. The word ״כִּי״ thus means ״even though״ (אף על פי כן), and the verse says: he crossed his hands even though Menasheh was the firstborn, so that his right hand rested upon Ephraim." "<b>And the correct interpretation</b> in my eyes is according to what the verse previously informed us — that Israel's eyes had grown heavy with age, so he could not recognize who was the firstborn and who was the younger. Therefore, when he wished to bless them, he understood in his mind that the one arranging them would arrange them in the customary manner, placing the firstborn at the right of the one blessing; and through this he knew each one's position and did what his thought had devised. This is the sense of the verse: ״שִׂכֵּל אֶת יָדָיו״ — he guided his hands with insight. Now if you should ask: from where did he know and recognize each one's place? Therefore it says ״כִּי מְנַשֶּׁה הַבְּכוֹר״ — that Menasheh was the firstborn; and from this he knew that he would be at the right, and therefore he guided his hands accordingly."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 48, :notes {0 "The Hebrew of [0] uses abbreviated phrasing with וגו׳ (and so forth); I have expanded it slightly to render the compressed argument intelligible, following the plain sense of his citation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:50:05.159107348Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}